[
  "[SCENE: Cam on desk setup, MacBook visible]\nI want to start with this sticker.\n[SHOW: Close-up of Dead Rabbit Radio sticker on MacBook lid]\nRight there on the lid of the MacBook Air, next to the Apple logo.\nIt came from Dead Rabbit Radio, a podcast I listened to while working on a transcription experiment.\nThis is Studio Workbench. I'm Mike Hall.\nI built a system that spread transcription work across five Raspberry Pis.\nI'll show what it did, where I stopped, and what I reused later.\n",
  "[SCENE: Show one of the original UGtastic recordings.]\nI had more than two hundred interviews on disk and no practical way to search them.\nPaying someone to transcribe the full collection was outside the budget for this community project.\nThe automated services I tried struggled with software names.\nI wanted to find out whether local speech recognition could help.\n[SHOW: A short example of the old transcript, if available.]\n",
  "[SCENE: Show whisper.cpp and the Fluffle repository.]\nIn 2023, I started trying whisper.cpp on my own hardware.\nI did not want my first tests to be on the UGtastic archive.\nI chose Dead Rabbit Radio because it had many episodes with one regular speaker.\nThat gave me a useful test set before I worked on the interviews.\n",
  "[SCENE: Show the Fluffle repository and the Raspberry Pi setup, if available.]\nI had five Raspberry Pis at home.\nRuby scripts divided the recordings, sent work to the boards, and collected the output.\nEach board ran whisper.cpp, then waited two minutes before taking another file.\n[SHOW: The distribution script and one worker command.]\nIt was a small system, built to answer a practical question: could these boards get through the test set?\n",
  "[SCENE: Show the completed transcript folder, then return to camera.]\nThe repository now has more than a thousand matching text and subtitle files.\nI contacted Jason to explain the experiment and ask whether he was comfortable with it. He replied kindly.\nI then tried to make the tool work with many different podcast feeds.\nFeed formats and audio metadata varied, and I had other work that needed my attention.\nI stopped working on the broader tool.\n",
  "[SCENE: Show a current UGtastic transcript beside its source recording.]\nFluffle did not become a universal podcast tool.\nIt gave me practice with distributing work and checking transcripts against the audio.\nI used some of that experience when I later returned to the UGtastic recordings.\nThanks to Jason for making the show and for being kind when I contacted him about the experiment.\nNext, I look at a field recording that I initially thought was out of sync.\n"
]